## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
{# skeleton-variant: check-platforms #}
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the `ansible-test` sanity sessions and the
  container platforms of Molecule. See
  [`extensions/molecule/README.md`](./extensions/molecule/README.md) for
  virtual machines and the execution environment scenario.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

Run the checks from the collection's Git root. With
[nox installed](#prerequisites), `nox` replaces `uv run noxfile.py`.

{# skeleton-variant: check-commands #}
```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # fix, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint  # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule -- test --platform-name molecule-debian13
```
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Among the default sessions:

- `runtime-check` installs the built collection in isolation and keeps only
  what ships: `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
{# skeleton-variant: runtime-scope #}
  imports, module documentation, and role loading and syntax, then runs the
  unit tests against that installation. Molecule covers how the roles deploy.
  To check the exact archive of a [release](#releases):

{# end skeleton-variant #}
  ```sh
  uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
  ```

{# skeleton-variant: deployment-lint #}
- `ansible-lint` runs with foundata's rules from `ansible-lint-foundata`,
  after checking that the rules the configuration enables are active. With
  `nox --no-venv` and the extension missing, it warns and runs only the stock
  rules.
{# end skeleton-variant #}
- `helper-lint` checks the Python outside `plugins/` with
{# skeleton-variant: helper-role-path #}
  [ruff.toml](./ruff.toml): `extensions/molecule/`, the roles' `files/`,
{# end skeleton-variant #}
  `tools/`, `scripts/` and `tests/` apart from `tests/unit/`,
  `tests/integration/` and `tests/output/`. Add it to CI configurations that
  list sessions explicitly.

Put the collection's own pytest suites below `tests/unit/` so ansible-test
runs them. Other Python below `tests/` is linted by `helper-lint` but is not
run as a test suite.

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](./noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](./antsibull-nox.toml).
{# skeleton-variant: check-iteration #}
Run one `molecule` session at a time; two runs at once collide on container
names. While editing, run the sessions of one ansible-core release, such as
`uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity-2.21` (`-l` lists them), and the
default sessions before a commit.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Below a deep checkout, nox gives each session a short symbolic link as
`TMPDIR`, because Unix socket paths are limited in length. A test of code that
refuses symbolic links in a path resolves `TMPDIR` first.

## Troubleshooting

To recover a short-path directory left by a killed Nox invocation, find it as
`nox.*` below `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, or below `/var/tmp` without one;
`uv run noxfile.py` leaves two. Read its `owner.json` at the exact path. It
records the path, the creator's host, PID and creation time and the directory's
device and inode. On that host, the invocation ended if no process has the PID
or the process holding it started after the creation time. Also confirm that
none of your processes has the path in its environment or working directory,
because a session's children outlive Nox. Compare the path without following
links. It holds only `owner.json` and numbered links to temporary directories.
Delete those entries by name, keep the link targets, and remove the directory
with `rmdir`. Leave symlinked or replaced paths and uncertain creators for
investigation. Nox itself removes only the unchanged directory its invocation
created.

## Releases<a id="releases"></a>

Each change users notice adds a fragment to `changelogs/fragments/`;
`00-fragment-template.txt` lists the sections. `release` turns the fragments
into `CHANGELOG.rst` and `changelogs/changelog.yaml`, so neither file is edited
by hand.

Releases require `releasing>=7.0.0` for the `release` command. Install the
development requirement in the maintainer's Python environment:

```sh
python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

`release build` runs the committed revision's Ansible sanity tests before
building the archive. Set `repository` in `.releasing.toml` before the first
release.

